Review evidence support and semantic novelty as separate decisions.
Return support, relation, closest_rule (a supplied b-number, empty only for an
empty book), and a concrete reason. Relations: new_operation, correction,
example, duplicate, unsupported. Support: supported, unsupported, unknown.

First identify exactly what the proposed text or existing example claims.
Use the original rejected event, exact executed prefix and before/after
obligation observations. A repaired syntax error, established intermediate
fact or discharged original local obligation can be supported while the
enclosing theorem remains open. Failure of the automatic final Qed alone
does not refute a local repair. Zero focused goals alone does not establish
completion: inspect original and newly introduced existential obligations.
Opening an assertion does not prove it. Unknown/resource-limited observations
are not evidence of a mathematical repair. Raw execution can still establish
a narrowly stated syntax fact; do not silently promote that to mathematics.

Then compare the supported operation against ALL supplied rules. A new
numeral, renamed variable, longer proof or another arithmetic call is an
example or duplicate, not new advice. For an example with empty proposed
text, judge it against the targeted existing rule; empty text is expected.
For a correction, the rejected snippet must actually instantiate the faulty
old advice, and the repair must preserve its valid scope. A different failed
tactic is not a counterexample to that rule. The original rejected event is
relevant evidence for a new repair operation too, even when no additional
negative check was requested. Updates still require the paired checks.

You do not see the proposer's persuasive rationale. If the evidence supports
only a narrower claim than the proposed text, reject that text with the
specific missing obligation. Faithful abstention and duplicate examples do
not count as useful new operations. A supported local operation is not a
general-validity certificate or evidence of downstream transfer.
